{
	title: 'Icon candidates demo page',
	spec: 'src/web/demo/icons.html, served at /demo/icons, compares fourteen candidate web-client icons (send, attach, image, thinking, download, copy, bash, read file, edit file, edit/pencil, web, pause/continue, steer, queue) across seven free sets: Lucide (ISC), Phosphor, Tabler, Hugeicons free, Fluent UI System Icons, Boxicons (MIT) and MingCute (Apache 2.0). Licence notices sit in page source; no attribution shows to users. Lucide, Phosphor and Tabler SVGs come from their repos; the others from the Iconify JSON packages. The user switches set (also via #lucide, #hugeicons and so on), stroke and size; the size slider (12–48px) scales every icon, and stroke affects line-drawn sets only. Pickers choose the thinking icon (real thought bubble by default, only in Hugeicons, Fluent, Boxicons and MingCute; light bulb, sparkle, chat bubble, ellipsis, atom, brain), steer icon, queue icon and attach icon (paper clip by default, or image plus), remembered in localStorage. A dashed box marks an icon a set lacks. Samples show composers (including pause, queue and steer while a turn runs) and transcript cards in the real theme colours, with a pencil edit button on a sent prompt. Icon-only buttons carry aria-labels. The page is a design aid; adopting icons in the client is a separate decision.',
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Icon data in icons.html is generated: one key table maps each icon to its name per set, and the SVG bodies are inlined as one JSON line (ICONS) plus NAMES. Iconify API search rate-limits quickly (HTTP 429); the jsdelivr @iconify-json/<prefix>/icons.json packages carry the same data without limits. Lucide, Phosphor and Tabler lack a true thought bubble (their chat bubbles with dots read as messaging).'
	],
	needs: ['86']
}
